A realistic script-level report where variable rules meet: a :PUBLIC
(info), an assignment to MYUSERNAME, an undeclared read and an unused
local (strict), and the predefined CRLF and MYUSERNAME reads, which are
declared by every base installation.
-- input.ssl --
:PARAMETERS sReportName;
:PUBLIC gReportRuns;
:DECLARE sHeader, sBody, nLines, sScratch;
gReportRuns := gReportRuns + 1;
sHeader := sReportName + " by " + MYUSERNAME + CRLF;
nLines := nRowCount;
sBody := sHeader + "lines: " + LimsString(nLines);
MYUSERNAME := "report-bot";
:RETURN sBody;
-- format --
:PARAMETERS sReportName;
:PUBLIC gReportRuns;
:DECLARE sHeader, sBody, nLines, sScratch;
gReportRuns := gReportRuns + 1;
sHeader := sReportName + " by " + MYUSERNAME + CRLF;
nLines := nRowCount;
sBody := sHeader + "lines: " + LimsString(nLines);
MYUSERNAME := "report-bot";
:RETURN sBody;
-- diagnostics --
8:1-8:11 warning global_assignment: Assignment to global variable 'MYUSERNAME' changes it for every routine that reads it
-- diagnostics [--strict --info] --
2:1-2:8 info limit_public_vars: ':PUBLIC' variables persist across procedures and risk namespace pollution. Prefer ':DECLARE' with parameter passing
3:34-3:42 hint unused_variable: Variable 'sScratch' is declared but never used
6:11-6:20 warning undeclared_variable: Variable 'nRowCount' is not declared
